The Security Council,
Reaffirming its strong commitment to the sovereignty, independence, unity and territorial integrity of Syria and to the purposes and principles of the Charter of the United Nations,
Recalling its resolutions 2042 (2012), 2043 (2012), 2118 (2013), 2139 (2014), 2165 (2014), 2175 (2014), 2191 (2014), 2209 (2015), 2235 (2015), 2254 (2015), 2258 (2015), 2268 (2016), 2286 (2016), 2332 (2016), 2336 (2016), 2393 (2017), 2401 (2018), 2449 (2018), 2504 (2020), 2533 (2020), 2585 (2021) and its presidential statements of 3 August 2011 (S/PRST/2011/16), 21 March 2012 (S/PRST/2012/6), 5 April 2012 (S/PRST/2012/10), 2 October 2013 (S/PRST/2013/15), 24 April 2015 (S/PRST/2015/10), 17 August 2015 (S/PRST/2015/15), and 8 October 2019 (S/PRST/2019/12),
Determining that the devastating humanitarian situation in Syria continues to constitute a threat to peace and security in the region,
Expressing grave concern at the deepening humanitarian crisis in Syria, including record levels of food insecurity, displacement, water scarcity, and the impact of the COVID-19 pandemic, and at the devastating impact of the conflict on civilians, including women, children, persons with disabilities, older persons, and other vulnerable groups,
Expressing deep concern at the finding of the Office of the United Nations High Commissioner for Human Rights that 306,887 civilians were killed in Syria between March 2011 and March 2021,
Condemning all violations of international humanitarian law and human rights law, including attacks against civilians, medical personnel, humanitarian workers, schools, hospitals and civilian infrastructure, and underlining the need to ensure accountability,
Reaffirming the obligation of all parties to respect international humanitarian law and the United Nations guiding principles of humanitarian emergency assistance, including humanity, neutrality, impartiality and independence,
Emphasizing the need for full, safe and unhindered humanitarian access, without delay, to all parts of Syria, including through cross-border and cross-line operations, and noting that humanitarian activities are broader than solely addressing immediate needs and should include support to essential services through water, sanitation, health, education and shelter early recovery projects,
Welcoming efforts to improve cross-line deliveries of humanitarian assistance and calling for meaningful and sustained progress, including to areas not reached for extended periods,
Stressing the importance of the full, equal and meaningful participation of women in all humanitarian and peace processes and in the design and delivery of humanitarian assistance, and the need to ensure protection from sexual and gender-based violence,
Recalling the need for all parties to respect the safety and security of United Nations personnel and humanitarian workers,
Noting with concern the continuing military escalation and hostilities in several parts of Syria, and the serious impact on civilians and humanitarian access,
Recalling that the authorization in resolution 2585 (2021) expires on 10 July 2022, and recognizing the urgent need for continued cross-border humanitarian operations to save lives,
Underlining the importance of transparency, efficiency and effectiveness in humanitarian operations, and of strengthened monitoring and reporting to ensure that assistance reaches those in need,
1. Demands the full and immediate implementation of all provisions of all relevant Security Council resolutions, including resolutions 2139 (2014), 2165 (2014), 2191 (2014), 2258 (2015), 2332 (2016), 2393 (2017), 2401 (2018), 2449 (2018), 2504 (2020), 2533 (2020) and 2585 (2021);
2. Decides to extend the decisions in paragraphs 2 and 3 of resolution 2165 (2014) for a period of six months, that is, until 10 January 2023, only for the border crossing at Bab al-Hawa, and decides that this authorization shall be extended for an additional six months, that is, until 10 July 2023, subject to the issuance of the Secretary-General’s substantive report, with particular focus on transparency in operations and progress on cross-line access in meeting humanitarian needs, and to the adoption of a further resolution by the Security Council;
3. Requests the Secretary-General to provide a substantive report by 10 December 2022, and to brief the Council monthly, on the implementation of this resolution, including on progress on cross-line deliveries, the volume and nature of assistance delivered through the Bab al-Hawa crossing, the number of beneficiaries, operating partners, distribution mechanisms, locations of aid deliveries at district level, and any impediments to humanitarian access, including bureaucratic obstacles;
4. Calls upon all parties to ensure full, safe, rapid and unhindered access for humanitarian personnel and supplies to all parts of Syria, without discrimination, including through cross-line and cross-border routes, and to facilitate the safe, voluntary and dignified return of refugees and internally displaced persons;
5. Demands that all parties comply with their obligations under international humanitarian law and human rights law, protect civilians and civilian objects, including medical facilities, schools, water and sanitation systems, and humanitarian personnel, and refrain from attacks on civilians and from the use of starvation as a method of warfare;
6. Calls upon all parties to take concrete steps to enhance the protection of civilians, including women and children, and to prevent and respond to sexual and gender-based violence, and stresses the importance of the full, equal and meaningful participation of women in the design and delivery of humanitarian assistance;
7. Calls for all parties to release persons arbitrarily detained, in particular women and children, and to provide information on the fate and whereabouts of missing persons;
8. Encourages all Member States to contribute to the United Nations humanitarian response plan for Syria and to support early recovery projects, including through water, sanitation, health, education and shelter, in accordance with humanitarian principles, and welcomes the efforts of the United Nations, the International Committee of the Red Cross and other humanitarian organizations;
9. Requests the Secretary-General to include in his reports information on the overall trends in United Nations cross-line operations, on the implementation of early recovery projects, and on measures taken to strengthen transparency, accountability and monitoring of humanitarian assistance, including to prevent diversion;
10. Calls upon all parties to facilitate the safe and unhindered delivery of humanitarian assistance, and to cooperate fully with the United Nations and humanitarian actors, including by simplifying administrative procedures and ensuring timely issuance of visas and permits;
11. Reaffirms its strong commitment to the sovereignty, independence, unity and territorial integrity of Syria, and calls upon all parties to refrain from actions that undermine the sovereignty and territorial integrity of Syria;
12. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.

