5. Demands that all parties comply with their obligations under international humanitarian law and human rights law, protect civilians and civilian objects, including medical facilities, schools, water and sanitation systems, and humanitarian personnel, and refrain from attacks on civilians and from the use of starvation as a method of warfare, and calls upon all parties to cooperate fully with relevant international mechanisms, including the Organisation for the Prohibition of Chemical Weapons;

6. Calls upon all parties to take concrete steps to enhance the protection of civilians, including women and children, and to prevent and respond to sexual and gender-based violence, and stresses the importance of the full, equal and meaningful participation of women in the design and delivery of humanitarian assistance;

7. Calls for all parties to release persons detained without legal basis, in particular women and children, and to provide information on the fate and whereabouts of missing persons;

8. Encourages all Member States to contribute to the United Nations humanitarian response plan for Syria and to support early recovery projects, including through water, sanitation, health, education and shelter, in accordance with humanitarian principles, welcomes the efforts of the United Nations and other relevant humanitarian actors, and calls for addressing early recovery and reconstruction needs, consistent with Syria’s sovereignty, independence, unity and territorial integrity;

9. Stresses that unilateral sanctions and other coercive measures should not impede humanitarian access or the delivery of humanitarian assistance, and calls upon all Member States to ensure that humanitarian activities are not adversely affected by such measures, including through appropriate exemptions and simplified procedures, consistent with international law;

